Here is the commit message:
Add block/position convention to all distinct from operators

This commit adds a second implementation to distinct from operator of all
types. This 2nd implementation uses block/position convention. As a
result, for certain types, it's no longer necessary to extract the value
out of the Block in order to invoke "distinct from" operator. This is
expected to resolve performance issue.

Here is the root cause:
The `IS_DISTINCT_FROM` operator for various SQL types (INTEGER, VARCHAR, IPADDRESS, TINYINT, TIME_WITH_TIME_ZONE, etc.) was only implemented using the "value + null-flag" calling convention (i.e., `@SqlType` scalar value plus `@IsNull boolean`). This forced the query engine to always materialize/extract the actual value out of the underlying `Block` before invoking the comparison, even when the values reside directly in blocks during execution (e.g., in join/aggregation/grouping paths that operate on `Block`/position pairs). For variable-length or boxed types such as `VARCHAR` (`Slice`), this extraction step incurs significant overhead (object allocation, copying), causing a measurable performance regression when `IS_DISTINCT_FROM` is evaluated repeatedly over blocks. The absence of a block/position-based overload meant the operator resolution framework could not select a more efficient comparison path that operates directly on `Block` data, which is the root cause of the performance bug being fixed.

